{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1147322-justification-for-an-imp-familiar","id":1147322,"title":"Justification for an Imp familiar?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-10-05T21:55:47-07:00","id":1147322,"title":"Justification for an Imp familiar?","content":"If you actually have an Imp familiar, how do you keep everyone from declaring you an Infernalist and killing you and destroying your soul? Why would a Mage want one and could the thing pursue a course of action not intended to do the most harm to the world? I'm rereading Forged by Dragon's Fire, and this is bugging me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-05T23:17:00-07:00","id":1147330,"title":"","content":"You could justify it by saying that it's not literally a demon from Actual Hell, but a spirit from the Astral Umbra, created from mankind's idea of an imp. In essence, the platonic manifestation of a diminutive devil, rather than a true inhabitant of the pit.<br /> <br /> If that doesn't fly, you could always say that it IS a demon, but you've bound it so heavily that it must obey. Binding demons into service - forcing their cooperation - has a long history. Though, obviously, not a clean one. Some will question if the imp is truly bound, or if the bindings are tight enough to prevent mischief or worse.<br /> <br /> As for why a mage would want an imp Familiar...they make for great spies, and are eager to increase their standing in the demonic Lowerarchy. Serving a stint as Familiar could afford the imp opportunities to do evil while its master isn't looking, or slowly tempt or trick them into greater infernalism. Or simply make the mage indulge their baser nature, confident that stoking the magician's pride will eventually lead them swallowed by Hell from pure sinfulness. So I think some imps would readily seek such servitude, for the opportunities it affords.<br /> <br /> Or, if the imp isn't a literal demon, it could be a spirit of general mischief, and acquiesce to Familiar-hood out of boredom or curiosity.<br /> <br /> Back to the mage, they may like the imp's skill set. They are tricksters and sneaks, commonly capable of illusion, invisibility, and general information gathering. It's also easier to bargain or bully into service than greater demons, and more eager to serve than more prideful kinds of spirits. Mostly because the imp has an agenda, and must be kept on a short leash.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-06T01:18:51-07:00","id":1147342,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mercurial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1147322-justification-for-an-imp-familiar#post1147322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you actually have an Imp familiar, how do you keep everyone from declaring you an Infernalist and killing you and destroying your soul? Why would a Mage want one and could the thing pursue a course of action not intended to do the most harm to the world? I'm rereading Forged by Dragon's Fire, and this is bugging me.</div> </div> </div> </div>The distinction between a demon that's just a spirit filtered through your paradigm and a demon that gets you accused of infernalism is incredibly weird and vague in Mage. The way I handled it in my home games is that any entities capable of enslaving or harming Avatars are Infernalism-type demons, regardless of their origin, behavior and intentions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12892-kammerer","name":"Kammerer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12892_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1191"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}